You must review current models and decide which existing entities must become tenant-owned, such as:
- packages
- blogs
- SEO configs
- pages
- media
- header config
- future inquiry/contact/email settings

Rules:
- every tenant-owned record must include tenantId
- every backend query for tenant-owned data must be tenant-scoped
- tenant isolation must be enforced in backend services/controllers, not only UI

==================================================
8. PHASE 3 — DOMAIN RESOLUTION LAYER
==================================================

Design and implement tenant resolution logic.

The platform must eventually support:
- subdomain routing
- custom domain routing

Examples:
- client1.platform.com
- client2.platform.com
- www.clienttourism.com

You must implement:
- hostname parsing
- tenant lookup by subdomain/custom domain
- tenant context injection into request lifecycle
- frontend tenant bootstrap logic

Create a clean tenant resolver module.

Expected responsibilities:
- detect current hostname
- resolve tenant
- fail gracefully if tenant not found
- expose tenant context to frontend and backend logic

==================================================
9. PHASE 4 — THEME ENGINE
==================================================

Build a configurable theme engine, but keep it simple and scalable.

Goal:
One shared component system should visually adapt per tenant.

Implement:
- CSS variable based theme handling or equivalent tokenized theme approach
- tenant theme loading
- theme fallback defaults
- runtime safe theme application

Theme properties should include at minimum:
- primaryColor
- secondaryColor
- textColor
- headingColor
- headingFont
- bodyFont
- borderRadius
- cardRadius
- buttonRadius
- spacing scale
- shadow level

Requirements:
- global theme per tenant
- optional future section-level overrides
- no raw arbitrary CSS fields from admin
- only controlled schema-based theme values

==================================================
10. PHASE 5 — SECTION REGISTRY FOUNDATION
==================================================

Build the base architecture for a section-driven page builder.

Important:
Do NOT build 40 sections.
Start with 6 to 8 solid reusable sections.

Initial recommended sections:
1. Hero
2. Featured Packages
3. Destinations / Highlights
4. About / Why Choose Us
5. Testimonials
6. Blog Preview
7. Gallery
8. Call To Action / Contact Section

Each section must be registered in a central SectionRegistry.

Each section definition should include:
- key
- label
- category
- supported variants
- default props/config
- supported style controls
- data source type
- admin editing schema

Example section capabilities:
- Hero supports headline, subheadline, CTA, media, alignment, overlay
- Featured Packages supports source, limit, layout variant, card style
- Blog Preview supports latest/fixed posts, layout variant, excerpt controls
- Gallery supports image source, grid style, columns

==================================================
11. PHASE 6 — PAGE BUILDER DATA MODEL
==================================================

Implement a page composition system.

Each page should be represented as an ordered list of section instances.

Model design should include something like:

PageConfig:
- id
- tenantId
- pageType
- slug
- title
- sections[]
- seo metadata
- status

PageSection:
- id
- type
- variant
- order
- enabled
- dataConfig
- styleConfig
- contentConfig

Requirements:
- page can contain 5 to 8 sections initially
- admin can reorder sections
- admin can enable/disable sections
- admin can select section variants
- page rendering must read from config, not hardcoded layout

==================================================
